course: dictionary
lesson: 1344
title: Campfire
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A campfire is a fire built outdoors at a camp, used for warmth, light and cooking. The word can also mean the gathering around that fire, with stories and songs. The words around it give you the clue: «Stay about three big steps away from a campfire» tells you it is a fire you can sit near, but it can still hurt you.
explain: Context can also tell you which meaning fits. «We sat around the campfire singing songs until bedtime» is about the gathering, not the fire itself. Try your meaning in the sentence: "We sat around the outdoor fire singing songs" still makes sense, so the first meaning works here.
explain: Some sentences give you a warning. «A boy in Canada ran around the campfire playing tag and tripped» shows that a campfire is hot and can cause burns. Words like tripped, embers and burns are clues that safety matters.
fact: A campfire is an outdoor fire made at a camp.
fact: Campfires give warmth and light, and can be used for cooking.
fact: Around the campfire means sitting close to the fire, often for stories and songs.
fact: Burning needs fuel, oxygen and heat; this is called the fire triangle.
fact: Never leave a campfire alone, and put it out completely by drowning it, stirring it and feeling for heat.
exercise: «Stay about three big steps away from a campfire.» What is a campfire? | A kind of song sung at school | A cold tent with no fire inside | A metal spoon that gets hot | *A fire made outdoors at a camp for warmth, light and cooking
exercise: Ben says, «We sat around the campfire telling stories.» What does campfire mean in this sentence? | A bushfire far away in the forest | A camp with no tents | A fire made in a kitchen | *A gathering around a fire, with stories and songs
exercise: Which of these is a safety rule for a campfire? | Sleep right next to it | Run around it while playing tag | *Never leave it alone | Pour petrol on it to make it burn faster
test: «A burn from a campfire should go under cool running water for 20 minutes.» What should you do first? | Ignore it until it stops hurting | Put ice or butter on the burn | Wrap it in a towel and keep playing | *Put the burn under cool running water and tell a grown-up
test: Which three things does a fire need, according to the fire triangle? | Sugar, salt and flour | Snow, rain and ice | Water, sand and wind | *Fuel, oxygen and heat
exam: What is the safe way to put out a campfire completely? | Leave it to burn down by itself | Pour petrol on it and step back | *Drown it, stir it and feel for heat | Cover it with dry leaves and go to sleep
